function e(e){return e.filter(e=>!!e.marker).map(e=>{let t=e.text.indexOf(`. `),n=t>0?e.text.slice(0,t+1):e.text,r=n.length>60?`${n.slice(0,60).replace(/[\s,;:]+$/,``)}…`:n;return{key:e.marker,text:r}})}var t={id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp`,title:`Paternity leave`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-A`,marker:`A`,text:`Attitudes towards paternity leave (陪产假) have drastically changed in America in the last five years as more fathers feel comfortable taking extended time off, but gender bias persists when it comes to career prospects and the home, according to a new study of working parents.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-B`,marker:`B`,text:`Research by the Boston College Center for Work & Family, which surveyed new parents at four large US companies who were qualified for taking at least six weeks paid parental leave, found that 81% of the 1,240 employees surveyed said the notion of fathers taking leave has become more acceptable.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-C`,marker:`C`,text:`Of those surveyed, 62% of fathers took the maximum amount of time off compared to 93% of mothers, and around three-quarters of workers said their employer was equally supportive of mothers and fathers taking parental leave and over half said leave policies had made workplace culture better.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-D`,marker:`D`,text:`The US is one of only three countries in the world not to offer statutory (法定的) paid leave, but increasingly states and companies are starting to take up the issue. So far, eight states and the District of Columbia have their own paid family leave laws.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-E`,marker:`E`,text:`Brad Harrington, executive director of the center and lead author of the study, estimates only 20% to 30% of companies in the US offer paid parental leave. He feels the research findings reflect an obvious change in corporate attitudes to new fathers taking time off.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-F`,marker:`F`,text:`"We did a study on paternity leave five years ago. Compared with those findings, these numbers were shocking to me. I did not expect 80% of people to say the organisation finds dads taking this leave acceptable and three-quarters to say it's equally supportive of women and men taking leave," Harrington said.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-G`,marker:`G`,text:`The previous study found that nearly three-quarters of fathers saw two to four weeks as an appropriate duration for paternity leave and 76% said they would prefer not to take all their time off at once.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-H`,marker:`H`,text:`Since then, there have been a number of legal cases against companies involving paternity leave - including cases against JPMorgan Chase and Estee Lauder - which have helped put pressure on companies to make their parental leave policies gender neutral.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-I`,marker:`I`,text:`However, the study also shows how traditional gender roles endure both at work, where more women than men reported changes in their perceived career potential, and at home - even among workers who claim to have a strong desire for equality.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-J`,marker:`J`,text:`The vast majority of men, 97%, said one of the top reasons to take leave was to share caregiving with their partner. But when they were asked about how caregiving and household tasks were divided, their answers painted a different picture. While about 75% of employees said both genders should give the same amount of care, the majority of men and nearly half of women admitted that in reality the female actually did most of it. A tiny fraction, 2%, of men said they did more of the childcare.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-K`,marker:`K`,text:`Men and women's experiences of the return to work following parental leave were also considerably different. Of the women surveyed, 32% reported a downturn in their job satisfaction, while 14% said it increased. In comparison, 17% of men said their job satisfaction went down and 20% said it went up. Meanwhile, more women reported an increase in their responsibilities and manager expectations after childbirth. Half of the women said they used flexible work arrangements after becoming a parent, while just 27% of men did. Similar percentages of men and women said they enjoyed their careers and that it gave them a sense of achievement, while around half of women and 44% of men said it was a key part of their identity.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-L`,marker:`L`,text:`On the subject of career advancement, 59% of women and 49% of men said leave could be limiting and both genders said they feared it would have an impact on their progress long-term. But on opportunity for promotion, more than double the number of women, 30% compared with 15% of men, believed their chances to be lower after becoming a parent. Despite progress, the struggle for women to reach the highest positions of power is demonstrated in this year's Fortune 500 list, which featured a record 33 female CEOs, but this still represents a tiny fraction of the total.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-M`,marker:`M`,text:`Harrington said culture change depends on companies putting more focus on men and their responsibilities. "By that I mean companies need to give men paternity leave and encourage men to take time off to be with their kids early on in the kids' life. They also need to recognise that men have to make significant adjustments when they become parents. Companies cannot do all these things to enhance women's advancement and then turn around and say, 'Oh, but we don't expect the men to take over for the women at home.'"`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-N`,marker:`N`,text:`In May, the American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU) and Outten & Golden LLP announced a historic class action $5m settlement with JPMorgan Chase on behalf of male employees who claim they were illegally denied access to paid parental leave. Derek Rotondo, 35, filed the discrimination charge against his company after he was allegedly told by his HR department that mothers were considered primary caregivers. Thus, they were allowed to take 16 weeks of paid parental leave. Fathers, however, could take just two weeks.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-O`,marker:`O`,text:`The father of two from Columbus, Ohio, who still works at the company as an associate and investigator, said he has witnessed a "domino effect (多米诺效应)" across companies since the settlement, but that there is still substantial progress to be made towards changing attitudes towards paternity leave.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-P`,marker:`P`,text:`"I do think there's still some way to go... there's still going to be sort of the unstated expectation for new dads to essentially come right back to work, but I think the research is showing that's starting to change." He said equal parental leave is an essential component to creating gender equality in the workplace. "The old standard of women staying home, having babies and cooking doesn't apply and hasn't applied for a long time."`}]},n={"cet4-2023-12-s2":{subtitle:`2023 年 12 月 · 第 2 套`,minutes:125,words:1400,tags:[`真题`,`四级`],summary:`2023年12月四级第2套，写作考家乡发展，翻译考改革开放后中国饮食变化。`,parts:[{id:`writing`,kind:`writing`,title:`Part I 写作`,instructions:`Directions: Suppose the university newspaper is inviting submissions from the students for its coming edition on the recent development in their hometown. You are now to write an essay for submission. You will have 30 minutes to write the essay. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.`,minutes:30,reference:{essay:`My hometown, once a quiet and unassuming town, has undergone a remarkable transformation in recent years. The once sleepy streets are now bustling with activity, and the skyline has been dramatically altered with the construction of new high-rise buildings. The economic boom has brought with it an influx of new businesses and industries, which have not only created job opportunities but also revitalized the local economy. Moreover, the development of public transportation has made it more convenient for residents to travel within the city and to neighboring towns. The new metro system, in particular, has significantly reduced traffic congestion and improved the quality of life for many. In conclusion, the recent development in my hometown has been nothing short of impressive. It has not only modernized the city but also provided a better living environment for its residents. I am excited to see what the future holds for this once quiet town.`,guidance:[`第1段：一句话点题。以 "My hometown, once a quiet and unassuming town, has undergone a remarkable transformation in recent years" 开篇，用 once…has undergone… 的今昔对比引出"家乡近年发生巨变"，直接呼应题目要求 the recent development in their hometown。`,`第2段前半：具体写变化。The once sleepy streets are now bustling with activity、skyline has been dramatically altered with new high-rise buildings——从街道繁华、高楼林立写城市面貌之变；economic boom brought an influx of new businesses and industries 写经济繁荣、产业涌入与就业机会；先总后分，画面感强。`,`第2段后半：落到民生改善。the development of public transportation has made it more convenient…、The new metro system… has significantly reduced traffic congestion and improved the quality of life——以交通与地铁为例写发展给居民生活带来的便利，用 Moreover、in particular 衔接，使"发展"更具体可信。`,`第3段：总结升华。In conclusion, the recent development… has been nothing short of impressive，再用 not only modernized the city but also provided a better living environment 概括意义，末句 I am excited to see what the future holds 展望未来收尾；三段结构，词数控制在 120–180。`]},questions:[{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-w1`,number:1,type:`writing`,stem:`Suppose the university newspaper is inviting submissions from the students for its coming edition on the recent development in their hometown. You are now to write an essay for submission. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`校报征文类作文，唯一任务点是"家乡近年的发展"。开篇用 once…has undergone a remarkable transformation 引出今昔对比；主体段先从街道、天际线写城市面貌，再从经济、就业写产业繁荣，最后用 Moreover、in particular 引出交通与地铁，落到居民生活质量；末段 In conclusion 总结，not only…but also… 概括意义并展望未来。三段结构，词数 120–180，语气积极、以具体变化支撑主题。`,score:15}]},{id:`listening`,kind:`listening`,title:`Part II 听力理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, you will hear three news reports. At the end of each news report, you will hear two or three questions. Both the news report and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations. At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions. Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear three passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:25,audio:{note:`站内合成朗读（非考试原音）`,segments:[{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-1`,label:`News Report 1`,questionNo:1,text:`A police officer in the U.S. stopped a large SUV car that was going very slowly and drifting across lanes on a highway. He expected to find a driver who was either very drunk or having a medical emergency. Instead, the officer discovered a five-year-old boy sat on the edge of the driver's seat. His feet could barely reach the brake, and his head was only just high enough to see out of the windows. The child had taken the keys to the family car while his teenage sister was napping. He then drove three kilometers across town before getting on the highway. The boy later told confused officers that he was planning to travel to California and buy a Lamborghini sports car. Although he only had three dollars in his wallet, at least he was driving in the right direction.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-2`,label:`News Report 2`,questionNo:3,text:`Mobile phones have changed the way we live. How we read, work, communicate, and shop. But we already know this. What we have not yet understood is the way the tiny machines in front of us are changing our skeletons, possibly altering not just the way we behave, but even the very shape of our bodies. New scientific research at the University of the Sunshine Coast in Queensland, Australia, suggests that young people are developing extra pieces of bone at the backs of their heads. These pieces of bone are caused by the way people bend their heads when they use the phone. This shifts weight onto the muscles at the back of the head and causes the bone to grow in a way that is not normal. This process can be compared to the way the skin hardens in response to constant rubbing or pressure. The result is a piece of bone like a horn that sticks out from the head by the neck.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-3`,label:`News Report 3`,questionNo:5,text:`A village is going to throw a birthday party for an orange cat named Wilbur who has become famous for making a regular appearance at local shops. Wilbur has his own internet pages as he approaches his 10th birthday on July 7. Wilbur is both bold and friendly. He spends most of his time in shops and businesses around the village. He'll just take himself into any shop anywhere. There was one time when the doctor's receptionist came back and he was sitting on her chair. Among his favourite haunts are a local hairdresser, two pubs and the Nottingham Primary School. Leslie Harper, who runs one of the pubs, said the cat has been a big hit with their customers. He's been a regular visitor for most of this year, she told the Press Association. He's a very relaxed cat, happy for customers and villagers young and old to come in and say hello. She also said he is a cat of expensive tastes who likes his high-priced cat food. Sarah Godfrey, who is organising Wilbur's party, told the local newspaper Wilbur is part of our lives as he is for everyone who lives in the village.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-4`,label:`Conversation 1`,questionNo:8,text:`So, where do you want to go for lunch? I don't know. Do you have anything in mind? What about the sandwich place on Camden Street? No, sorry. I don't feel like sandwiches today. It's a great place, but I think we go there too often. That's true. Remember, that's where we saw Bridget Clark, the famous movie star. Of course. How could I ever forget? There were crowds of people around her, asking for a photo with her. What about hamburgers, then? There's that American-style diner on the way to the mall. I like that place. Their chips are great, but their service takes a long time, and I need to get back by 2:30 for a conference call. How about trying something new? We could try Mario's, the new Italian restaurant on the corner. It looks alright. Have you been there? No, I haven't, but I've noticed that it has great reviews on the Internet. It's supposed to be one of the best Italians in town. I think Jeremy has been there and said it was amazing. I'm up for that. Cool. Have you asked Jeremy if he would like to come with us? I already have. He says he can't. He has brought his own lunch today, so he will stay in. I think his wife made him soup. Poor Jeremy. His wife is a terrible cook. He should throw that soup in the bin and join us.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-5`,label:`Conversation 2`,questionNo:12,text:`Hi there. How are you today? Do you have a reservation with us already? Good afternoon. Yes, we reserved our rooms yesterday morning on your website for three nights. The name's Patterson. OK, let me have a look. Yes, we have it here. You brought the whole family with you, I see. Yes, the two kids, my wife and I, and her parents too. Great. So, we have a family room for you and your wife and the kids, and another double room for your parents-in-law. They are right next to each other on the ground floor, since you mentioned in your message that they have trouble with stairs. That's wonderful. My father-in-law has had terrible problems getting up and down stairs since his knee operation last April. I'm sorry to hear that. And if you need any help to find transportation for the whole family, we can definitely recommend someone for you. We were thinking of renting a car, but we will explore all the options available for sure. So yes, that would be very helpful in comparing prices. We're also wondering what tours and day trips are available. We have bunches of brochures here. I would recommend getting out on a boat trip. The kids will love it, and there are so many islands nearby to explore. There's also a great night market further into town that has all kinds of food and cool little shops selling souvenirs and local jewelry and clothing made by hand. That all sounds marvelous. Now, all I need is to photocopy your passports, and then I can get you all checked in and show you to your rooms.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-6`,label:`Passage 1`,questionNo:16,text:`Artificial intelligence agents play ever more influential roles in our lives. They do everything from suggesting new friends to recommending purchases. They're even beginning to drive our cars. Another role that they are expected to take over is negotiating on our behalf in commercial transactions or legal disputes. So, it's important to know whether using an artificial intelligence agent might affect how we negotiate. Research indicates that it does. In a new study, participants were told to imagine that they were negotiating for something important to them, like a house. Next, they were told either that they would negotiate for themselves, or they would program an artificial intelligence agent to negotiate for them. Participants were then asked to indicate how tough they would be in the negotiation. Those who had programmed an AI agent to negotiate on their behalf were far more likely to use deceptive strategies, such as hiding information or making false claims, than those negotiating for themselves. The researchers say this is because people feel less personally responsible for the actions of an agent they have programmed, and are more willing to let it behave unethically.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-7`,label:`Passage 2`,questionNo:19,text:`New research has studied the effects of a seven-week healthy cooking course. Academics measured the programme's effect on cooking confidence and self-perceived mental health. Researchers also measured participants' overall satisfaction around cooking and diet-related behaviours. What were the findings? Course participants saw significant improvements in general health. They also reported improved mental health and subjective vitality immediately after the programme. These benefits remained six months after the completion of the course. What caused these improvements? Researchers have previously found a link between eating more fruits and vegetables and improved longer-term mental health. This would imply that the participants in the current study may have felt better due to improved diet. However, the study showed participants' mental health improved even if their reported diet did not change after completing the programme. Also, the mental health benefits were equal among participants who were overweight or obese and those in a healthy weight range. This suggests a link between cooking confidence and satisfaction around cooking and mental health benefits. Who benefits most from learning to cook? Gender plays a part. At the start of the programme, 77% of female participants were confident about cooking, but just 23% of males were confident. At the end of the programme, cooking confidence and skills were equal across both counterparts. This change in confidence could lead to a gender balance in home cooking. This, in turn, could reduce consumption of unhealthy, high-calorie processed meals.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-8`,label:`Passage 3`,questionNo:22,text:`What is personal space? We often think of it as an invisible bubble of space surrounding us that others can't enter without causing discomfort. Research shows, however, that we actually have bubbles of different sizes. Each of these bubbles applies to a different set of people. The smallest zone, called intimate space, extends outward from our bodies 18 inches in every direction. Only family, pets, and one's closest friends may enter it. A mere acquaintance entering our intimate space makes us uncomfortable. Next is the bubble called personal space, extending from 1.5 feet to 4 feet away. Friends and acquaintances can comfortably occupy this zone, especially during informal conversations, but strangers are forbidden. Extending from 4 to 12 feet away from us is social space. Here, people feel comfortable conducting routine social interactions with new acquaintances or total strangers. Those are the average sizes of Americans' personal bubbles, anyway. It is important to keep in mind that personal space varies depending on culture and context. Furthermore, there are significant individual differences. As we all know, cultural or individual differences in personal bubble diameters are all too often the cause of discomfort. How did these personal bubbles arise? According to research, we begin to develop our individual sense of personal space around age 3 or 4. The sizes of our bubbles are fixed by our teens. These bubbles are constructed and monitored by the brain region involved in fear.`}]},questions:[{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l1`,number:1,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-1`,stem:`Why did the police officer stop the SUV car?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) He noticed the driver was too young to drive.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) He found there was no one sitting at the wheel.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) He thought something must be wrong with the driver.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) He saw the driver changing lanes much too frequently.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "He expected to find a driver who was either very drunk or having a medical emergency"——警察见车缓慢行驶、偏离车道，以为司机要么醉驾要么突发疾病，即断定司机一定出了问题，选 C；D) 频繁变道只是表面现象，并非拦车动机。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-1`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l2`,number:2,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-1`,stem:`What did the boy tell the police officers he was planning to do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Buy a sports car.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Drive across town.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Leave California.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Visit his sister.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "he was planning to travel to California and buy a Lamborghini sports car"——男孩说要去加州买一辆兰博基尼跑车，题干问"计划做什么"，选 A；穿越城镇是已发生的事实，不是计划。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-1`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l3`,number:3,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-2`,stem:`What does the report say we have not yet understood about mobile phones?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) How they change the way we shop.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) How they alter human skeletons.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) How they cause increased headaches.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) How they affect our communication.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "What we have not yet understood is the way the tiny machines in front of us are changing our skeletons"——我们尚未弄明白的是手机正在改变人的骨骼，选 B；改变购物、交流方式是 "we already know this" 的已知影响。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-2`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l4`,number:4,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-2`,stem:`What happens to the skin when rubbed or pressed constantly?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It loosens.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It brightens.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It hardens.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It softens.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "This process can be compared to the way the skin hardens in response to constant rubbing or pressure"——皮肤在持续摩擦或挤压下会变硬，用于类比头骨异常生长，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-2`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l5`,number:5,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-3`,stem:`What does the report say the village is going to do for the cat Wilbur?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Create Internet pages for him.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Ask a local pet shop to adopt him.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Name an orange tree after him.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Hold a birthday party for him.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "A village is going to throw a birthday party for an orange cat named Wilbur"——村子要为这只橘猫办生日派对，选 D；网络页面是 Wilbur 早已有之，并非村庄要做的事。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-3`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l6`,number:6,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-3`,stem:`What do we learn from the report about the cat?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) He is a bold and aggressive pet.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) He pays regular visits to village shops.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) He once bit a doctor's receptionist.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) He likes to sit on the hairdresser's chair.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "He spends most of his time in shops and businesses around the village" 且被称为 "a big hit with their customers"——它常去村里各家店铺，选 B；A) aggressive 与原文 "bold and friendly" 不符，C、D 均属曲解。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-3`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l7`,number:7,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-3`,stem:`What does the pub owner, Leslie Harper, say about the cat?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) He is fond of luxury cat food.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) He likes to stay in villagers' houses.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) He knows everybody in the village.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) He often seeks food around her pub.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "he is a cat of expensive tastes who likes his high-priced cat food"——酒吧老板说它品味昂贵、喜欢高价猫粮，即喜爱高档猫食，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-3`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l8`,number:8,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-4`,stem:`What are the speakers talking about in this conversation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Who to order the food.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Whether to have sandwiches.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) When to go for their meal.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Where to have their lunch.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`对话开头 "So, where do you want to go for lunch?"，随后依次讨论三明治店、美式餐厅和新开的意大利餐厅，全程围绕午餐地点，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l9`,number:9,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-4`,stem:`Where did the speakers see Bridget Clark, the famous movie star?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) In the shopping center nearby.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) In the expensive Italian style diner.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) At the Sandwich Place on Camden Street.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) At the American restaurant they frequent.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "Remember, that's where we saw Bridget Clark, the famous movie star" 中 that's 指上一句的 "the sandwich place on Camden Street"，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l10`,number:10,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-4`,stem:`Why does the woman say she needs to get back by 2:30?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) There is to be a conference call.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) She has to meet with her boss then.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) There will be crowds of people waiting for her.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) She will have a photo taken with Bridget Clark.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "I need to get back by 2:30 for a conference call"——女士要在 2:30 前赶回参加电话会议，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l11`,number:11,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-4`,stem:`Why does the woman say Jeremy should throw his soup in the bin?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) She doesn't deem homemade soup tasty.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) She doesn't think his wife cooks well.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) She feels Jeremy would rather dine out.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) She has found the soup smells terrible.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "His wife is a terrible cook. He should throw that soup in the bin and join us"——女士认为 Jeremy 的妻子厨艺太差，所以该把汤倒掉，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l12`,number:12,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-5`,stem:`Who is the man talking to in the conversation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) A landlady.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) A waitress.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) A receptionist.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) A saleswoman.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`对方开口 "Do you have a reservation with us already?"，随后查预订、安排房间、复印护照办理入住，可判断是酒店前台接待员，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l13`,number:13,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-5`,stem:`What do we learn about the man's father-in-law from the conversation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) He was involved in a terrible car accident last April.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) He has much difficulty getting up and down stairs.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) He is expected to undergo a knee operation.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) He prefers to stay next door to the children.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "My father-in-law has had terrible problems getting up and down stairs since his knee operation last April"——岳父上下楼梯十分困难，选 B；膝盖手术是去年四月已做完，C) 说"将要手术"不对。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l14`,number:14,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-5`,stem:`Why does the man say they will explore all the options available?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) To please his parents-in-law.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) To find the best trip for his kids.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) To satisfy his curiosity.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) To compare prices.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`前台表示可推荐交通方式，男士回应 "that would be very helpful in comparing prices"——了解各种选择是为了比价，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l15`,number:15,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-5`,stem:`What does the woman suggest the man and his family do close to the end of the conversation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Visit a local art gallery.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Go on a boat trip.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Take some photos of the islands.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Try her hand-made clothing.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "I would recommend getting out on a boat trip. The kids will love it"——前台建议一家人乘船出游，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l16`,number:16,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-6`,stem:`What does the passage say about artificial intelligence agents?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They are more intelligent than many of us.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They have already become our new friends.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They have begun to affect our social behavior.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They play increasingly more important roles.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "Artificial intelligence agents play ever more influential roles in our lives"——智能体扮演越来越重要的角色，ever more influential 即 increasingly more important，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-6`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l17`,number:17,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-6`,stem:`What does the new study want to find out about using an artificial intelligence agent?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Whether it might have any effect on the way we negotiate.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Whether it might actually outperform human negotiators.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Whether it can facilitate business transactions.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Whether it can speed up legal procedures.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "it's important to know whether using an artificial intelligence agent might affect how we negotiate. Research indicates that it does"——研究旨在弄清智能体是否影响谈判方式，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-6`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l18`,number:18,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-6`,stem:`What did participants tend to do when assigned an AI agent to negotiate on their behalf?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Choose to be tough.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Sympathize with their opponent.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Use deceptive strategies.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Appear to be pleasant.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "Those who had programmed an AI agent to negotiate on their behalf were far more likely to use deceptive strategies, such as hiding information or making false claims"——让智能体代谈者更倾向使用欺骗策略，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-6`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l19`,number:19,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-7`,stem:`What do we learn about the benefits the participants gained from the Healthy Cooking course?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They were perceived differently by some academics.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They still existed six months after the course ended.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They varied greatly among the course participants.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They were only measurable within seven weeks.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "These benefits remained six months after the completion of the course"——健康烹饪课程的益处课结束后仍持续六个月，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-7`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l20`,number:20,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-7`,stem:`What do the new research findings suggest about mental health benefits?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They can be easily seen among participants in a healthy weight range.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They should be attributed to participants' change in diet behaviours.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are linked to cooking confidence and cooking satisfaction.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They actually result from eating more fruits and vegetables.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "This suggests a link between cooking confidence and satisfaction around cooking and mental health benefits"——心理健康益处与烹饪信心、对烹饪的满意度相关，选 C；原文明确饮食未变也有改善，B、D 被否定。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-7`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l21`,number:21,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-7`,stem:`What plays a role in determining who benefits most from learning to cook?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Gender.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Confidence.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Health.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Practice.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "Who benefits most from learning to cook? Gender plays a part"——谁从学做饭中受益最多，性别起作用，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-7`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l22`,number:22,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-8`,stem:`What does research show about our personal space?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It keeps others away.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It causes discomfort.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It remains visible.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It varies in size.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "we actually have bubbles of different sizes. Each of these bubbles applies to a different set of people"——研究显示个人空间气泡大小不一，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-8`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l23`,number:23,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-8`,stem:`What happens if a mere acquaintance enters our intimate space?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It makes us feel uncomfortable.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It renders the acquaintance a stranger.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It brings the acquaintance closer to us.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It causes the bubble around us to vanish.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "A mere acquaintance entering our intimate space makes us uncomfortable"——普通熟人进入亲密空间会让我们不适，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-8`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l24`,number:24,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-8`,stem:`Where do people feel comfortable interacting with new acquaintances or strangers?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) In personal space.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) In social space.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Within a distance of 18 inches.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Over 2 feet away from one another.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "Extending from 4 to 12 feet away from us is social space. Here, people feel comfortable conducting routine social interactions with new acquaintances or total strangers"——社交空间里与新熟人或陌生人交往自在，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-8`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-l25`,number:25,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-8`,stem:`When are the sizes of our bubbles fixed?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) When we begin to feel fear.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) When we develop a sense of space.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) When we are 3 or 4 years old.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) When we enter our teens.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "The sizes of our bubbles are fixed by our teens"——气泡大小在十几岁时固定，选 D；3、4 岁只是开始形成个人空间感的年龄，C 为干扰项。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-ls-8`},score:1}]},{id:`reading`,kind:`reading`,title:`Part III 阅读理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Directions: In this section, you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2. Directions: There are 2 passages in this section. Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements. For each of them there are four choices marked A), B), C) and D). You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:40,bank:{A:`boost`,B:`case`,C:`casually`,D:`efficiency`,E:`estate`,F:`exhausted`,G:`faculty`,H:`interfere`,I:`normal`,J:`prevalent`,K:`relative`,L:`removed`,M:`scratch`,N:`shaped`,O:`surprisingly`},passages:[{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-cp`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-cp-1`,text:`A number of studies have looked at how family life can affect productivity and satisfaction in the workplace. However, there has been [26] little research on the influence of leisure activities. So Ciara Kelly and colleagues recruited 129 hobbyists to look at how the time spent on their hobbies [27] their work life.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-cp-2`,text:`The researchers found that when participants spent longer than [28] on their leisure activity, their belief in their ability to perform their job was strengthened. But this was only the [29] when they had a serious hobby that was dissimilar to their job, or when their hobby was similar to their work but they only did it [30]. When their hobby was both serious and similar to their job, then spending more time on it actually decreased their work [31]. Why might that be? To maintain a serious hobby, people need to invest significant psychological resources, say the authors - so if the activity has the same kinds of demands as their work, they may be left [32] and unable to perform well at their job.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-cp-3`,text:`But if their hobby is quite different from their career, it may not [33] in the same way but instead help them develop other knowledge and skills that can [34] their confidence at work. "Consider a scientist who is a keen rock climber," says Kelly, "Since climbing is so far [35] from their day-to-day work activities, they can still recover from the demands of their job with plenty of resources."`}]},t,{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p1`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p1-1`,text:`Having a rival can keep you committed to achieving your goals and enhance your overall performance. But before you go out and find an entrepreneur to outcompete, it's important to understand and avoid the traps that often come with rivalry. After all, competitive rivalry can also hinder effective decision-making and increase your willingness to take risks, behaviors that can ultimately hurt your venture's success.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p1-2`,text:`Finding someone you're committed to outcompeting can be a great way to stay focused on your goals and push your venture to the next level. But when you're intently focused on outperforming your rivals, you may begin to develop a "win-at-all-costs" mentality that causes you to ignore how you achieve success.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p1-3`,text:`One group of researchers, for example, examined the link between rivalry and unethical behavior. They found that when people compete against their rivals, they are more willing to behave unethically to win. But such behavior may stain your reputation and strain relationships important to your success. One way to avoid this trap is to stop and reflect on what's important. While outperforming your rivals may provide short-term benefits, the loss of your integrity will have long-term consequences.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p1-4`,text:`One reason having a rival can enhance your venture's performance is that it creates a level of excitement that drives you to work harder. But this eagerness to win may also hurt your venture's success, particularly when it causes you to make impulsive, insensible decisions. But it's possible to avoid such costly mistakes by making a habit of engaging in critical thinking, such as considering opposing viewpoints and conducting cost-benefit analyses, especially for those decisions that are complex and can determine the future of your venture.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p1-5`,text:`The sense of eagerness that comes with having a rival can not only cause you to make poorer decisions, but it can also lead you to take greater risks that put your venture in peril. One way you can overcome the risk inducing effects of rivalry that stand to endanger your venture's success is to remain attentive to your emotional state and actively monitor how such feelings are affecting your decision-making.`}]},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p2`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p2-1`,text:`A multitasker is one who can perform two or more tasks effectively at the same time, which - apart from the obvious differences - is similar to what a computer does. The concept does indeed come from the realms of technology, where it is used to refer to an operating system that can execute multiple tasks at the same time.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p2-2`,text:`However, the question is: can a person really be a multitasker? For most scientists, the answer is no. So much so that, according to experts in neuroscience (神经系统科学), our brains do not handle multitasking situations well. As soon as two tasks require our attention, productivity suffers.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p2-3`,text:`What we call multitasking, therefore, is in reality the ability to move more or less quickly from one task to another. This requires two essential conditions: that one of the tasks needs to be automatic, like walking or eating, and that they both need different mental processes. Answering the phone and writing at the same time, for example.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p2-4`,text:`However, on the other side of the coin there are people who maintain that it is possible to be, or at least seem to be, multitasking. A recent study concluded that regardless of whether people are actually handling several tasks or not, the mere fact that they perceive this activity as multitasking has a positive effect on their performance.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p2-5`,text:`The business perspective offers a different view: multitasking is understood as the ability to adapt to all types of environment within a company and effectively undertake different activities within a set time frame. Indeed, many companies look for people who are skilled in multitasking to improve their productivity. From this different perspective, you can not only be multitasking but this ability can also be taught: something that is easier in fluid organisations, which favour flexibility in their working practices.`},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p2-6`,text:`The benefits of multitasking are clear. Being quicker and more efficient increases our performance and the number of tasks completed. But having to pay attention to several things at once means that the powers of concentration are reduced and that can lead to more mistakes.`}]}],questions:[{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-c26`,number:26,type:`bank`,stem:`However, there has been __26__ little research on the influence of leisure activities.`,answer:`O`,explanation:`surprisingly 出人意料地：关于休闲活动影响的研究"出人意料地少"，there has been surprisingly little research 中修饰 little 需副词，选 O。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-cp-1`,quote:`However, there has been [26] little research on the influence of leisure activities.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-c27`,number:27,type:`bank`,stem:`…to look at how the time spent on their hobbies __27__ their work life.`,answer:`N`,explanation:`shaped 塑造、影响：研究花在爱好上的时间如何"影响"其工作生活；how 引导的宾语从句中需谓语动词过去式，选 N。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-cp-1`,quote:`to look at how the time spent on their hobbies [27] their work life.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-c28`,number:28,type:`bank`,stem:`…when participants spent longer than __28__ on their leisure activity…`,answer:`I`,explanation:`normal 正常水平：参与者在休闲活动上花费的时间"超过正常水平"时，工作能力信念增强；longer than normal 为常见搭配，选 I。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-cp-2`,quote:`when participants spent longer than [28] on their leisure activity`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-c29`,number:29,type:`bank`,stem:`But this was only the __29__ when they had a serious hobby that was dissimilar to their job…`,answer:`B`,explanation:`case 情况：这种增强"仅限于"爱好认真且不与工作相似时；the case 为固定表达（this was only the case when…），选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-cp-2`,quote:`But this was only the [29] when they had a serious hobby that was dissimilar to their job`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-c30`,number:30,type:`bank`,stem:`…or when their hobby was similar to their work but they only did it __30__.`,answer:`C`,explanation:`casually 随意地：若爱好与工作相似但只是"随便做做"，也会有增强效果；修饰动词 did 需副词，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-cp-2`,quote:`their hobby was similar to their work but they only did it [30].`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-c31`,number:31,type:`bank`,stem:`…spending more time on it actually decreased their work __31__.`,answer:`D`,explanation:`efficiency 效率：当爱好既认真又与工作相似时，多花时间反而降低工作"效率"；work efficiency 搭配自然，且与首句 productivity 呼应，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-cp-2`,quote:`spending more time on it actually decreased their work [31].`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-c32`,number:32,type:`bank`,stem:`…they may be left __32__ and unable to perform well at their job.`,answer:`F`,explanation:`exhausted 筋疲力尽的：若爱好与工作要求相同，人可能"耗尽精力"、无法做好工作；left 后接形容词作主语补足语，选 F。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-cp-2`,quote:`they may be left [32] and unable to perform well at their job.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-c33`,number:33,type:`bank`,stem:`But if their hobby is quite different from their career, it may not __33__ in the same way…`,answer:`H`,explanation:`interfere 干扰：与职业迥异的爱好不会以同样方式"干扰"，反而帮助发展其他知识技能；may not 后接动词原形，选 H。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-cp-3`,quote:`it may not [33] in the same way but instead help them develop other knowledge and skills`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-c34`,number:34,type:`bank`,stem:`…skills that can __34__ their confidence at work.`,answer:`A`,explanation:`boost 增强、提升：其他知识与技能能"增强"工作信心；can 后接动词原形，boost one's confidence 为常见搭配，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-cp-3`,quote:`other knowledge and skills that can [34] their confidence at work.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-c35`,number:35,type:`bank`,stem:`Since climbing is so far __35__ from their day-to-day work activities…`,answer:`L`,explanation:`removed 远离的：far removed from 为固定搭配，意为"与……相去甚远"，攀岩与科学家的日常工作相距甚远，故选 L。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-cp-3`,quote:`Since climbing is so far [35] from their day-to-day work activities`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-m36`,number:36,type:`single`,stem:`In the absence of Federal legislation, some states in the US have passed laws concerning paid family leave.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`D`,explanation:`D 段 "The US is one of only three countries in the world not to offer statutory (法定的) paid leave… eight states and the District of Columbia have their own paid family leave laws"——联邦层面无带薪假立法，部分州自行立法，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-D`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-m37`,number:37,type:`single`,stem:`Most fathers admitted that even during their paternity leave they actually did much less childcare than the mother.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`J`,explanation:`J 段 "the majority of men and nearly half of women admitted that in reality the female actually did most of it. A tiny fraction, 2%, of men said they did more of the childcare"——多数父亲承认实际育儿中母亲做得更多，选 J。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-J`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-m38`,number:38,type:`single`,stem:`According to one father, equal parental leave is indispensable to achieving gender equality in the workplace.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`P`,explanation:`P 段 "He said equal parental leave is an essential component to creating gender equality in the workplace"——一位父亲称平等育儿假是实现职场性别平等的关键要素，essential component 即 indispensable，选 P。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-P`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-m39`,number:39,type:`single`,stem:`One survey indicated there is now less objection to paternity leave.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`B`,explanation:`B 段 "found that 81% of the 1,240 employees surveyed said the notion of fathers taking leave has become more acceptable"——调查显示父亲休产假变得更易被接受，即反对减少，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-B`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-m40`,number:40,type:`single`,stem:`Compared to five years ago, according to one researcher, many more people said their organisation gave the same support to men and women taking parental leave.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`F`,explanation:`F 段 "I did not expect 80% of people to say the organisation finds dads taking this leave acceptable and three-quarters to say it's equally supportive of women and men taking leave"——Harrington 对比五年前，惊讶于如今多数人称单位对男女休假同等支持，选 F。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-F`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-m41`,number:41,type:`single`,stem:`One study finds that even workers who claim to desire gender equality stick to traditional gender roles both at work and at home.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`I`,explanation:`I 段 "traditional gender roles endure both at work… and at home - even among workers who claim to have a strong desire for equality"——即便声称渴望平等者，传统性别角色依然延续，选 I。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-I`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-m42`,number:42,type:`single`,stem:`The majority of workers surveyed said parental leave policies had improved workplace culture.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`C`,explanation:`C 段 "over half said leave policies had made workplace culture better"——超过半数受访者称休假政策改善了职场文化，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-C`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-m43`,number:43,type:`single`,stem:`In spite of progress, the number of women in top positions of big companies remains extremely small.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`L`,explanation:`L 段 "Despite progress… a record 33 female CEOs, but this still represents a tiny fraction of the total"——尽管有进步，女性高管仍仅占极小比例，选 L。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-L`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-m44`,number:44,type:`single`,stem:`According to one estimate, less than one third of companies in the US provide paid parental leave.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`E`,explanation:`E 段 "estimates only 20% to 30% of companies in the US offer paid parental leave"——据估计仅 20%-30%（不足三分之一）的美国公司提供带薪育儿假，选 E。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-E`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-m45`,number:45,type:`single`,stem:`A number of lawsuits have pressured companies to formulate gender neutral policies on parental leave.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`H`,explanation:`H 段 "a number of legal cases against companies involving paternity leave… have helped put pressure on companies to make their parental leave policies gender neutral"——多起诉讼施压，促使公司制定性别中立的育儿假政策，选 H。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-mp-H`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-r46`,number:46,type:`single`,stem:`How can competitive rivalry benefit entrepreneurs according to the passage?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) By enabling them to outcompete other entrepreneurs.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) By enabling them to make their venture a success.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) By helping them to reach long-term goals.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) By helping them to stay goal-oriented.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`第二段首句 "Finding someone you're committed to outcompeting can be a great way to stay focused on your goals"——竞争能让创业者保持目标专注，stay goal-oriented 即 stay focused on your goals，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p1-2`,quote:`Finding someone you're committed to outcompeting can be a great way to stay focused on your goals and push your venture to the next level.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-r47`,number:47,type:`single`,stem:`What is one of the traps entrepreneurs may often fall into when competing with rivals?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They may adopt strategies that are bound to ruin their venture.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They may resort to unethical means to outperform their rivals.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They may be too eager to succeed while ignoring the huge labor cost.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They may be intently focused on winning at the current market level.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`第三段 "when people compete against their rivals, they are more willing to behave unethically to win"——陷阱之一是为胜过对手而不择手段，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p1-3`,quote:`They found that when people compete against their rivals, they are more willing to behave unethically to win.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-r48`,number:48,type:`single`,stem:`What are entrepreneurs advised to do to avoid traps that often accompany rivalry?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Deliberate on what really matters.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Prioritize reaping immediate benefits.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Estimate the long-term consequences.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Reflect on what successes are achievable.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`第三段 "One way to avoid this trap is to stop and reflect on what's important"——建议停下来思考什么才真正重要，Deliberate on what really matters 与之对应，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p1-3`,quote:`One way to avoid this trap is to stop and reflect on what's important.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-r49`,number:49,type:`single`,stem:`How can entrepreneurs avoid making impulsive and insensible decisions?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) By engaging themselves in critical reasoning.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) By developing a habit of keeping their integrity.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) By criticizing themselves for previous poor performances.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) By refraining from being too excited about their successes.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`第四段 "it's possible to avoid such costly mistakes by making a habit of engaging in critical thinking"——养成批判性思考的习惯可避免冲动、不明智的决定，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p1-4`,quote:`But it's possible to avoid such costly mistakes by making a habit of engaging in critical thinking, such as considering opposing viewpoints and conducting cost-benefit analyses.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-r50`,number:50,type:`single`,stem:`How can entrepreneurs overcome the risk-inducing effects of rivalry?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) By paying close attention to their current performance.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) By taking steps that stand to endanger their rivals' success.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) By monitoring how their decision-making impacts their mentality.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) By keeping their emotions in check to avoid making poor decisions.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`末段 "remain attentive to your emotional state and actively monitor how such feelings are affecting your decision-making"——要留意并管控情绪状态、监控其对决策的影响，即控制情绪以避免糟糕决策，选 D；C) 颠倒了"情绪影响决策"的方向。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p1-5`,quote:`One way you can overcome the risk inducing effects of rivalry that stand to endanger your venture's success is to remain attentive to your emotional state and actively monitor how such feelings are affecting your decision-making.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-r51`,number:51,type:`single`,stem:`What does a "multitasker" originally refer to?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) An operating system capable of doing several tasks at once.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) A skilled worker executing more than one task at the same time.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) A sophisticated technology doing several tasks effectively at once.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) An efficient person able to perform multiple tasks at the same time.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`第一段 "The concept does indeed come from the realms of technology, where it is used to refer to an operating system that can execute multiple tasks at the same time"——"multitasker" 原指可同时执行多项任务的操作系统，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p2-1`,quote:`where it is used to refer to an operating system that can execute multiple tasks at the same time.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-r52`,number:52,type:`single`,stem:`Why can't people really be multitaskers according to neuroscientists?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They are not sufficiently exposed to multitasking situations.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They are not comparable to mechanical operating systems.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Their brains do not allow them to multitask.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Their attention span cannot be expanded.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`第二段 "according to experts in neuroscience (神经系统科学), our brains do not handle multitasking situations well"——神经科学家认为人脑无法很好地处理多任务，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p2-2`,quote:`our brains do not handle multitasking situations well. As soon as two tasks require our attention, productivity suffers.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-r53`,number:53,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from the conclusion of a recent study on multitasking?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) People make greater achievements by maintaining whatever they are doing is multitasking.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) People's performance benefits from the perception of what they are doing as multitasking.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) People's active mental processes exert a positive effect on their multitasking.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) People can improve their capabilities by handling multitasking situations.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`第四段 "the mere fact that they perceive this activity as multitasking has a positive effect on their performance"——仅仅把活动看作多任务处理，就会对表现产生积极影响，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p2-4`,quote:`the mere fact that they perceive this activity as multitasking has a positive effect on their performance.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-r54`,number:54,type:`single`,stem:`How does the business world view multitasking?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It is a rare skill often found in fluid organisations.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It is an adaptable capability required of all workers.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It is an essential quality many employees lack.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It is a desirable ability that can be developed.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`第五段 "many companies look for people who are skilled in multitasking to improve their productivity" 及 "you can not only be multitasking but this ability can also be taught"——商界视其为值得拥有且可培养的能力，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p2-5`,quote:`From this different perspective, you can not only be multitasking but this ability can also be taught.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-r55`,number:55,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author imply we should do if we have to focus on some task and do it well?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Work in a flexible way.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Learn from mistakes.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Avoid multitasking.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Increase efficiency.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`末段 "having to pay attention to several things at once means that the powers of concentration are reduced and that can lead to more mistakes"——同时顾及多事会降低专注力、导致更多错误，作者暗示若要专注做好一件事应避免多任务处理，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-12-s2-p2-6`,quote:`But having to pay attention to several things at once means that the powers of concentration are reduced and that can lead to more mistakes.`},score:1}]},{id:`translation`,kind:`translation`,title:`Part IV 翻译`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to translate a passage from Chinese into English. You should write your answer on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:30,reference:{translation:`Since the reform and opening up, there have been significant changes in the diet of Chinese people. In the past, due to economic backwardness, food varieties were limited and supplies were insufficient, and people were just content with being able to eat their fill. Nowadays, with the rapid development of China's economy, food has become not only more diverse but also of significantly improved quality. As living standards continue to rise, people have increasingly high expectations of their diet and place greater emphasis on eating nutritious and healthy food. Consequently, low-fat, low-sugar, and organic foods introduced to the market are widely welcomed by people.`},questions:[{id:`cet4-2023-12-s2-t1`,number:1,type:`translation`,stem:`改革开放以来，中国人的饮食(diet)发生了显著变化。过去由于经济落后，食品种类有限、数量不足，人们仅仅满足于吃得饱。如今中国经济快速发展，食品不仅更加丰富多样，质量也大幅提高。随着生活水平不断提升，人们对饮食的要求越来越高，更加注重吃得营养健康。因此，目前市场上推出的低脂、低糖、有机食品受到人们的普遍欢迎。`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`要点：①"改革开放以来"译作 Since the reform and opening up，主句用 there have been significant changes in the diet of Chinese people。②"由于经济落后，食品种类有限、数量不足"译作 due to economic backwardness, food varieties were limited and supplies were insufficient；"满足于吃得饱"用 were just content with being able to eat their fill。③"不仅更加丰富多样，质量也大幅提高"用 not only more diverse but also of significantly improved quality；"随着生活水平不断提升"用 As living standards continue to rise。④"更加注重吃得营养健康"用 place greater emphasis on eating nutritious and healthy food；末句"受到人们的普遍欢迎"处理为 are widely welcomed by people，低脂、低糖、有机食品译为 low-fat, low-sugar, and organic foods。全文以现在完成时 / 一般现在时为主，注意中文短句的逻辑合并。`,score:15}]}]}};export{n as default};